{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-217287",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator $4s+3$ cannot be zero, since division by $0$ is undefined. Therefore we cannot have $4s+3=0,$ which means $s=-\\frac{3}{4}$ cannot be a solution.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2s}{4s+3} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2s\\cdot 5 & =(4s+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 10s & =8s+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10s & =8s+6 \\\\ 10s-8s & =8s+6-8s \\\\ 2s & =6 \\\\ s & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $s=3$ is different from $s=-\\frac{3}{4}$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $s=3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
